 ## Overview
-Gabor noise is a procedural noise function that generates band-limited, anisotropic or isotropic patterns by convolving a sparse set of random impulses with a Gabor kernel [S1, S3]. It solves the problem of visible kernel structures (artifacts) common in general sparse convolution noise at low densities, while providing explicit spectral control over orientation, frequency, and bandwidth [S1, S2]. Unlike its sibling [Locally Controlled Spot Noise](4599-locally-controlled-spot-noise.md), which allows spatial variation of kernel parameters, standard Gabor noise typically uses fixed or globally varied parameters to define a specific power spectrum [S1, S3].
+Gabor noise is a procedural noise function that generates band-limited, anisotropic or isotropic patterns by convolving a sparse set of random impulses with a Gabor kernel [S1][S2, unofficial][S3]. It solves the problem of visible kernel structures common in general sparse convolution noise at low densities, while providing explicit spectral control over orientation, frequency, and bandwidth [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Locally Controlled Spot Noise](4599-locally-controlled-spot-noise.md), which allows spatial variation of kernel parameters, standard Gabor noise typically uses fixed or globally varied parameters to define a specific power spectrum [S1, S3].
 
 ## How it works
-Gabor noise builds on the sparse convolution framework defined in [Spot Noise](2959-spot-noise.md). It replaces the generic kernel with a Gabor kernel, which is the product of a circular Gaussian and a 2D cosine wave [S1, S3].
+Gabor noise builds on the sparse convolution framework defined in [Spot Noise](2959-spot-noise.md) [S3]. It replaces the generic kernel with a Gabor kernel, which is the product of a circular Gaussian and a 2D cosine wave [S1, S3].
 
